/* AI4DM representation atlas (public) — GENERATED by build.py, do not edit by hand */
window.MX_ATLAS={"intro":"In architecture and landscape architecture, the rendering is one mode of representation among many, and historically a minor one. The discipline thinks through sketches, measured drawings, diagrams, scores, maps, models and collages, each invented to show something a photograph of the future cannot. AI image models default to rendering; this atlas maps the modes they overlook, anchors each in canonical work, and shows how to push the machine toward them.","modes":[{"id":"sketch","title":"Sketch","tagline":"thinking made visible, before it is right","definition":"A sketch is a fast, provisional drawing made to find an idea, not to present one. Its value lies in what it refuses to settle: broken edges, repeated strokes and visible corrections keep the design open to revision, and that openness is the point. Sketching is thinking with the hand; the drawing is a record of a conversation the designer is having with themselves. Because it commits to almost nothing, the sketch can hold contradictory options on one page in a way no finished drawing can.","purpose":["First contact with a site or program, when nothing is decided and everything must stay movable","Testing many alternatives cheaply: ten bad thumbnails teach more than one polished view","Recording observation in the field, where drawing forces slower and better looking than a photo","Thinking through a problem whose answer you do not yet know; the hand often finds it before the head","Keeping an idea deliberately unresolved so collaborators can still push it"],"conventions":["Speed over accuracy: line quality shows searching; corrected and repeated strokes stay visible","Economy of means: one pen or one pencil, cheap paper, no preciousness","Words are part of the drawing: annotations, arrows and question marks carry half the thinking","Series, not single images: the same idea drawn again and again until it resolves","Stop before it is finished; a sealed, complete sketch has quietly become a presentation drawing"],"canon":[{"who":"Laurie Olin","work":"Travel and site sketchbooks","year":"1970s-present","why":"decades of daily sketching as a discipline of observation: drawing a place to understand it before daring to change it","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurie_Olin","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Lawrence Halprin","work":"Sea Ranch site studies and water studies","year":"1960s","why":"sketching as environmental analysis: wind, fog, grazing and moving water studied stroke by stroke in notebooks","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Halprin","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Francis Halle","work":"Tropical Trees and Forests: An Architectural Analysis","year":"1978","why":"the architecture of a tree drawn to be understood: growth, branching and reiteration recorded as patient analytical line, sketching as the discipline of looking hard enough to see what a photograph flattens","tier":"internal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Hall%C3%A9"},{"who":"Kengo Kuma","work":"Asakusa Culture Tourist Information Center studies","year":"2012","why":"one parti, the stacked roofs, drawn dozens of times; iteration itself is the design method","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kengo_Kuma","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Peter Eisenman","work":"House IV axonometric permutations","year":"1971","why":"sketching as systematic transformation: a rule applied and redrawn in sequence, not a picture refined","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Eisenman","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Jim Lee (SWA Group)","work":"On sketching as internal dialogue","year":"n.d.","why":"names the mode's real function: the sketch is the designer talking to themselves, not performing for others","tier":"internal"}],"ai":{"helps":["Rapid variation: from one rough underlay it can spin out many alternative directions to react against","Loosening: it can roughen a hard-line drawing into a sketch register so early work reads as early","Unblocking: when you are stuck, a batch of generated thumbnails gives you something to disagree with"],"breaks":["AI cannot be genuinely unresolved: a generated 'sketch' is a finished design wearing a sketchy style; the searching never happened","It settles what you wanted open, committing to materials, planting and shadows a real study would refuse to decide","It adds detail where a sketch subtracts; every corner gets closed, every edge completed","The value of sketching is in the making, and delegating the making deletes the thinking; the image survives, the thought does not"],"recipes":[{"label":"Loosen a drawing","prompt":"Redraw this image as a loose graphite hand sketch on off-white paper. Keep the exact composition, footprints and proportions unchanged. Use searching, repeated pencil strokes with visible construction lines; leave edges broken and unresolved. No colour, no shading gradients, no rendering polish. Do not add any detail, texture, people or planting that is not in the source."},{"label":"Ten thumbnails","prompt":"Generate a single sheet of ten small, rough thumbnail sketches exploring different massing options for a small pavilion on a sloped riverside site. Fine-liner pen on white paper, each thumbnail no more than twenty strokes, deliberately unfinished, with short hand-drawn arrows and no words. Vary the parti between thumbnails; do not render materials, shadows or context."},{"label":"Five-minute site study","prompt":"Redraw this site photograph as a rapid felt-tip pen study, the kind made standing on site in five minutes. Thick confident strokes for the main masses, thin broken lines for edges still in question, a few arrows marking wind direction and key views. Keep the topography and the positions of existing trees exactly as in the photo. White background, no colour except one light grey marker wash, no rendering."}]},"lensPack":[["Loose graphite","as a loose graphite hand sketch, visible construction lines, deliberately unfinished"],["Pen thumbnails","as a sheet of rough pen thumbnails, maximum twenty strokes each, no words"],["Trace overlay","as a trace-paper overlay study: faint underlay, bold strokes only where the idea changes"],["Keep it open","leave edges broken and unresolved; do not settle anything the source leaves open"]],"examples":[{"id":"hand-drawn-plan-to-cad","title":"Hand-drawn site plan → clean CAD linework","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-watercolour","title":"Render → watercolour","tier":"public"},{"id":"sketch-to-massing-study","title":"Loose sketch → massing study","tier":"public"},{"id":"watercolour-plan-to-campus","title":"Watercolour plan → top-down render (school campus)","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"measured-drawing","title":"Measured Drawing","tagline":"every line is a real distance","definition":"The plan, section and elevation are the discipline's instruments of precision: drawings made at a declared scale, in which any dimension can be taken off with a ruler and trusted. They are contracts as much as pictures; a wall drawn 300 millimetres thick is a promise about the world. Lineweight and poche encode a strict grammar of what is cut, what is seen and what lies beyond, so that a trained reader reconstructs three-dimensional space from flat marks. No other mode carries legal, constructive and spatial authority in a single sheet.","purpose":["Fixing decisions exactly, so they can be priced, permitted and built","Coordinating with engineers, fabricators and contractors through a shared scale and shared conventions","Testing dimension against the body and the code: door widths, riser heights, turning radii, slopes","Holding a whole project consistent: the plan, the sections and the elevations must all agree"],"conventions":["A declared scale, kept absolutely: the drawing is measurable or it is not this mode","Lineweight hierarchy: heaviest for what the plane cuts, medium for profiles seen in elevation, lightest for what lies beyond","Poche: material cut by the plane fills solid black or hatches, reading instantly as mass against space","Plans conventionally cut about 1.2 metres above the floor, catching doors and windows","North arrow, scale bar, closed dimension strings; the sheet must survive photocopying and time"],"canon":[{"who":"Weiss/Manfredi","work":"Olympic Sculpture Park drawing set","year":"2007","why":"sections where landform, infrastructure and art are one continuous measured construction crossing a highway and a rail line","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Sculpture_Park","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Garrett Eckbo","work":"Community Homes cooperative plans","year":"1940s","why":"modernist landscape plans in which planting is dimensioned and composed with the same rigour as structure","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garrett_Eckbo","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Renzo Piano","work":"Chateau La Coste pavilion section","year":"2017","why":"a building held inside a terrain section: the ground is cut and measured with the same care as the roof","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_La_Coste","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","work":"Barcelona Pavilion plan","year":"1929","why":"the canonical demonstration that a plan is a spatial argument: eight cruciform columns and freestanding planes, legible at a glance","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona_Pavilion"},{"who":"Carlo Scarpa","work":"Brion Cemetery working drawings","year":"1969-1978","why":"measured drawing pushed to devotion: layered pencil on trace where every joint is dimensioned and every revision remains visible"}],"ai":{"helps":["Graphic dress rehearsal: restyling a finished CAD export to test presentation graphics before committing","Producing texture fills, vegetation symbols and entourage to collage into a real drawing","Turning a plan into an atmospheric teaser image for early client conversations, while CAD stays the source of truth"],"breaks":["AI has no unit system and cannot hold scale: a wall entered at 90 centimetres can return at 87, and nothing in the model knows or cares","'Every line represents a real distance' is precisely the promise a diffusion model cannot make; treat any AI output as an image of a drawing, never as a drawing","Conventions decay under generation: poche grows texture, lineweights blur into shading, dimension strings turn to garbled marks that do not close","It invents plausible-looking impossibilities: stairs with wrong riser counts, doors swinging through walls, walls that shift thickness mid-run"],"recipes":[{"label":"Plan graphics pass","prompt":"Restyle the graphics of this CAD plan export without altering its geometry: walls cut by the plan become solid black poche, floor areas a single pale grey tone, all vegetation as thin-line circles, everything else in uniform thin black line. Do not move, add or remove any element; do not add furniture, texture, shadows or perspective. Strict flat orthographic view, white background, no text."},{"label":"Section conventions","prompt":"Redraw this section as a hard-line orthographic section drawing. Everything cut by the section plane becomes solid black poche, including the ground, with a heavy ground line and solid earth fill below grade. Everything beyond the cut is drawn in thin uniform grey line. No perspective, no vanishing points, no shading, no material textures, no sky gradient. Keep all heights and proportions exactly as in the source; add nothing."},{"label":"Elevation flattening","prompt":"Convert this facade photograph into a flat orthographic elevation study: remove all perspective convergence so every horizontal edge is truly horizontal and every vertical is truly vertical, keep the real proportions of all openings, and draw in uniform thin black line with one heavier ground line. No textures, no shadows, no colour, no entourage. This is a proportional study only; it carries no dimensional authority."}]},"lensPack":[["Poche cut","everything cut by the plane rendered as solid black poche, space beyond in thin line"],["Three lineweights","exactly three lineweights: heavy cut, medium profile, light beyond"],["No perspective","strict orthographic projection, no convergence, no vanishing points"],["Flat white sheet","white background, no shadows, no textures, no colour, no text"]],"examples":[{"id":"aerial-to-linework-siteplan","title":"Campus aerial photo → linework site plan","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-brooklyn-brownstone","title":"Text → A1 presentation board (Brooklyn brownstone renovation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-street-renovation","title":"Text → landscape board (street renovation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"building-photo-to-front-side","title":"Building photo → front and side elevations (drawing style)","tier":"public"},{"id":"elevation-to-massing","title":"Elevation → volumetric massing","tier":"public"},{"id":"hand-drawn-plan-to-cad","title":"Hand-drawn site plan → clean CAD linework","tier":"public"},{"id":"interior-photo-to-section","title":"Interior photo → measured-style section","tier":"public"},{"id":"painting-to-orthographic-blueprint","title":"Building image → plan, elevation and section blueprint","tier":"public"},{"id":"pavilion-photo-to-front-side","title":"Pavilion photo → front and side views","tier":"public"},{"id":"perspective-to-cad-plan","title":"Perspective render → CAD-style plan drawing","tier":"public"},{"id":"plan-to-interior-render","title":"Floor plan → interior render (view from the red arrow)","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-drawing-set","title":"Library renders → full technical drawing set","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-elevation-svg","title":"Bath house render → CAD-style elevation (+ SVG step)","tier":"public"},{"id":"renders-to-library-board","title":"Project renders → competition board (library)","tier":"public"},{"id":"section-to-3d-isometric","title":"Landscape section → realistic 3D isometric","tier":"public"},{"id":"style-linework-to-illustration","title":"Linework isometric → flat colour illustration (palette input)","tier":"public"},{"id":"style-photoreal-to-red-linework","title":"Photorealistic render → red pen/linework illustration","tier":"public"},{"id":"villa-render-to-elevation-svg","title":"Villa render → CAD-style elevation (+ SVG step)","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"diagram","title":"Diagram","tagline":"say one thing, remove everything else","definition":"A diagram is an act of selection: it isolates one relationship, such as program, circulation, structure or view, and deletes everything that does not serve it. Its power comes from subtraction; what a diagram leaves out is as authored as what it keeps. A good diagram is an argument that can be grasped in three seconds and defended for an hour. Where the rendering asks to be believed, the diagram asks to be understood, and this makes it the mode most fundamentally at odds with generative imagery, which always wants to add.","purpose":["Isolating a single idea so it can be examined, criticised and compared without distraction","Explaining the parti to a jury or client in the first ten seconds of a presentation","Comparing alternatives on strictly equal graphic terms, one variable changing at a time","Thinking in relationships (adjacency, flow, hierarchy) rather than appearances"],"conventions":["One subject per diagram; a diagram that says two things says nothing","Flat solid fills, a deliberately small palette of two to four colours, no shadows, gradients or textures","A consistent symbol vocabulary: the same arrow means the same thing in every diagram of the set","Relationships must be accurate even when scale is suppressed; a lying diagram is worse than none","Serial discipline: the same base drawing repeated, with only the argued layer changing"],"canon":[{"who":"Christopher Alexander","work":"A Pattern Language","year":"1977","why":"253 problems each reduced to one small diagram: proof that a spatial argument can survive on almost no ink","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Pattern_Language","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Kevin Lynch","work":"The Image of the City","year":"1960","why":"paths, edges, districts, nodes, landmarks: an entire city compressed into five diagrammatic elements","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Image_of_the_City","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Venturi and Scott Brown","work":"Learning from Las Vegas","year":"1972","why":"the duck and the decorated shed: a two-sketch diagram that reorganised architectural theory","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_from_Las_Vegas","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Louis Kahn","work":"Philadelphia traffic movement studies","year":"1951-1953","why":"streets notated as flows with arrows and rhythms: the diagram as a way of seeing the city as movement, not fabric","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Kahn","tier":"internal"},{"who":"OMA / Rem Koolhaas","work":"Program bar diagrams (Downtown Athletic Club analysis; Seattle Central Library)","year":"1978-2004","why":"program as stacked coloured bars: quantity becomes form, and the diagram becomes the building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_for_Metropolitan_Architecture"}],"ai":{"helps":["Fast graphic variations of a diagram you have already structured: palette, weight and style tests","Cleaning and unifying hand-drawn diagram sketches into flat consistent graphics","Generating figure-ground and massing reductions from imagery as raw material to verify and redraw"],"breaks":["The central tension: diagrams remove while generative models add; trained to fill every pixel with plausible detail, AI resists emptiness and re-decorates what you abstracted","Asked for a diagram, it returns an infographic-styled rendering: gradients, soft shadows, little 3D extrusions and garbled pseudo-labels","It cannot decide what to omit, because omission is the design act; it does not know your argument, so it keeps everything","It flattens your specific argument into a generic 'diagram style' scraped from the internet, which is a look, not a logic"],"recipes":[{"label":"Flat circulation diagram","prompt":"Convert this plan into a circulation diagram. Flat solid fills and exactly four colours: white background, light grey building mass, one red for primary movement, dashed black for secondary movement. No shadows, no gradients, no textures, no 3D, no text of any kind. Remove all material detail, furniture, planting and dimensions; keep only the building outline and the movement lines as the subject. Movement lines are smooth, of even width, with arrowheads only at their endpoints."},{"label":"Program bars","prompt":"Redraw the program of this building as a stacked bar diagram in strict flat elevation: one horizontal band per floor, each program a flat solid colour block sized proportionally to its floor area, maximum five colours, thin black separating lines, white background. No perspective, no texture, no icons, no shadows, and no text labels; type will be added separately."},{"label":"Figure-ground","prompt":"Reduce this aerial image to a figure-ground diagram: all building footprints as solid black shapes, all open space white, and nothing else. No roads, no trees, no shadows, no grey tones, no outlines around shapes. Keep every footprint in its exact position and true proportion; do not merge, regularise or invent footprints."}]},"lensPack":[["Four flat colours","flat solid fills, maximum four colours, no shadows, no gradients, no texture"],["One subject only","keep only the named subject; delete everything that does not explain it"],["Figure-ground","solid black footprints on white, nothing else in the image"],["No text","no text, labels or numbers of any kind; type will be set later"]],"examples":[{"id":"board-oma-community-centre","title":"Text → A1 presentation board (OMA-style community centre)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-street-renovation","title":"Text → landscape board (street renovation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-waterfront-renewal","title":"Text → landscape board (waterfront renewal & erosion mitigation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"cad-to-exploded-axon","title":"Rhino screenshot → exploded isometric assembly diagram","tier":"public"},{"id":"massing-to-program-axon","title":"Massing render → colour-coded program axonometric","tier":"public"},{"id":"perspective-to-program-masterplan","title":"Perspective render → diagrammatic program masterplan","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-analysis-diagrams","title":"Library render → circulation, exploded and analysis diagrams","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-figure-ground","title":"Render → figure-ground diagram","tier":"public"},{"id":"renders-to-library-board","title":"Project renders → competition board (library)","tier":"public"},{"id":"section-to-3d-isometric","title":"Landscape section → realistic 3D isometric","tier":"public"},{"id":"spreadsheet-to-tree-masterplan","title":"Species spreadsheet → tree-placement masterplan diagrams","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-high-line","title":"Text → plan-view timeline (the High Line)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-oakridge-park","title":"Text → collage timeline (Oakridge Park redevelopment)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-sydney-opera-house","title":"Text → massing-evolution timeline (Sydney Opera House)","tier":"public"},{"id":"townhouse-plan-to-axon","title":"Townhouse landscape plan → 3D axonometric diagram","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"notation-scores","title":"Notation & Scores","tagline":"drawing what happens, not what stands","definition":"Notation gives graphic form to what conventional drawings cannot hold: time, movement, event, sound, smell, process. A score is a set of instructions for a performance that has not yet happened; it can be closed and exact, like Labanotation, or open and interpretable, like Halprin's Motation, which choreographs how people move through a designed landscape. Stan Allen's distinction anchors the mode: diagrams describe relationships in space, notations describe relationships in time. For landscape architects, whose material grows, floods and erodes, notation is not exotic; it is the honest drawing of the medium itself.","purpose":["Designing experiences that unfold: a procession, a tide cycle, a festival, a growing season","Recording the invisible layers of a site: movement patterns, sound, smell, rhythm of use","Scoring open-ended participation, where the design sets rules and people improvise the rest","Working across disciplines with choreographers, ecologists and musicians in a shared graphic language"],"conventions":["An explicit axis of time: left to right, top to bottom, or along a path, but declared and kept","A small glyph vocabulary with fixed meanings, drawn identically at every occurrence","A legend or key; notation without a key is decoration","Layered simultaneous tracks (movement, event, environment) aligned to the same timeline","Read-back test: another person must be able to perform or reconstruct the sequence from the score alone","Anticipation: a score describes a performance not yet realised, so it must be complete enough for another person to enact, not merely to admire (Stan Allen's first working definition of notation)"],"canon":[{"who":"Lawrence and Anna Halprin","work":"Motation and the RSVP Cycles","year":"1965-1969","why":"a notation system for movement through environments, built with a choreographer: scores as instruments of collective creativity","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Halprin","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Appleyard, Lynch and Myer","work":"The View from the Road","year":"1964","why":"the highway experience notated as sequence diagrams: space and attention scored against travel time","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Appleyard","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Rudolf Laban","work":"Labanotation","year":"1928","why":"the proof of concept: human movement recorded precisely enough to be re-performed decades later from paper alone","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labanotation","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Bernard Tschumi","work":"Fireworks; The Manhattan Transcripts","year":"1974-1981","why":"event, movement and space split into parallel notational tracks: architecture defined by what happens in it","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Tschumi","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Stan Allen","work":"Practice: Architecture, Technique and Representation","year":"2000","why":"the theoretical anchor: notations belong to time as diagrams belong to space"},{"who":"Jorinde Voigt","work":"Notational drawings","year":"2000s-present","why":"an artist's demonstration that rigorous self-invented notation can carry music, weather and thought on one sheet","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorinde_Voigt","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Kate McLean","work":"Sensory maps and smellwalks","year":"2010s","why":"smell, the least drawable sense, given graphic form through fieldwork-based notation","tier":"internal"}],"ai":{"helps":["Generating candidate glyph vocabularies and mark-making styles for you to select from and redraw","Producing visual variations of a score you have already structured, to test density and legibility","Illustrating single frames or moments of a sequence you have scored, as companion images"],"breaks":["AI has no model of duration: it renders a moment, and time collapses into style","Asked for a score, it produces decorative sheet-music-like graphics whose symbols mean nothing and cannot be read back","It cannot keep a glyph consistent: the same event marker mutates across the sheet, destroying the grammar","It invents notation without a key, which is the one unforgivable act in this mode: marks with authority but no meaning"],"recipes":[{"label":"Movement score","prompt":"Draw a horizontal movement score for a ten-minute walk through a park: a single ruler-straight timeline running left to right, divided into ten equal minutes. Above the timeline, one continuous line whose height records walking pace. Below it, small repeated glyphs marking events: one circle per pause, one triangle per framed view, one square per distinct sound. Flat black ink on white. Use only these three glyph types, drawn identically at every occurrence. No text, no perspective, no scenery, no decoration."},{"label":"Fixed-frame time series","prompt":"Generate a strip of six identical-format frames showing the same street corner at six moments of one day, with the camera fixed and unchanged in every frame. Only light, people and activity may change; the buildings, trees and road stay exactly identical across all six frames. Flat illustration style with a limited palette, no photorealism. Frames separated by thin black rules, no captions, no timestamps."},{"label":"Path notation","prompt":"Notate the sensory events along this walking route: redraw the route as a single continuous black line on a white ground, then place concentric dashed circles at each event point, radius proportional to intensity, in a maximum of three flat colours keyed to event type. No base map detail beyond the path line itself: no buildings, no roads, no labels, no legend. The key will be typeset separately."}]},"lensPack":[["Timeline strip","as a horizontal score: a straight timeline with repeated glyphs marking events, flat ink on white"],["Fixed-frame series","a strip of identical frames where only time changes; camera and geometry never move"],["Glyph discipline","use only the given glyphs, drawn identically at every occurrence; no invented symbols"],["Notation only","no perspective, no scenery, no rendering; marks on a timeline"]],"examples":[{"id":"circulation-overlay","title":"Render → circulation overlay","tier":"public"},{"id":"massing-to-program-axon","title":"Massing render → colour-coded program axonometric","tier":"public"},{"id":"perspective-to-program-masterplan","title":"Perspective render → diagrammatic program masterplan","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-analysis-diagrams","title":"Library render → circulation, exploded and analysis diagrams","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-high-line","title":"Text → plan-view timeline (the High Line)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-oakridge-park","title":"Text → collage timeline (Oakridge Park redevelopment)","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"projection-systems","title":"Projection Systems","tagline":"how we measure, not how we see","definition":"Orthographic, axonometric and oblique projections share one law: projectors are parallel, so parallel lines in the object stay parallel in the drawing and never meet. Nothing shrinks with distance, which means dimensions survive the trip onto paper; perspective shows how we see, parallel projection shows how we measure and construct. The convention is not a Western invention: Chinese scroll painting used ruled parallel projection to carry cities across metres of silk centuries before European drawing codified it. In the twentieth century the axonometric became an ideology, prized by Lissitzky and the avant-garde precisely because it refuses a privileged human viewpoint.","purpose":["Showing plan, height and depth in one measurable image, without perspective's distortions","Explaining assemblies and systems: the exploded axon is how architecture explains itself to itself","Depicting fields and cities without hierarchy, since no point is closer to the eye than any other","Carrying continuous narratives along a surface, as the scroll tradition demonstrates"],"conventions":["Parallel edges stay parallel everywhere in the image; a single converging pair breaks the mode","Declared construction: plan axon keeps the plan undistorted and rotated (commonly 45 or 30/60), verticals extruded at true height","One projection angle for the whole drawing, foreground and background alike","No depth cues from diminution, blur or haze; depth is carried by geometry alone","Exploded views separate along one axis with thin alignment lines preserving registration","Plan oblique, also called military projection, keeps the plan true to scale and extrudes verticals straight up: the landscape architect's workhorse for holding ground pattern and massing in one measurable image"],"canon":[{"who":"Francis D. K. Ching","work":"Architectural Graphics: the unfolding glass box","year":"1975","why":"the pedagogical primal scene: orthographic views explained as an object inside a glass box whose faces unfold into plan, section and elevation","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Ching","tier":"internal"},{"who":"El Lissitzky","work":"K. und Pangeometrie","year":"1925","why":"the axonometric argued as a worldview: parallel projection as the modern refusal of the fixed Renaissance eye","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Lissitzky","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Zhang Zeduan","work":"Along the River During the Qingming Festival","year":"early 1100s","why":"a city carried across five metres of silk in consistent parallel projection: the convention predates the West by centuries","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Along_the_River_During_the_Qingming_Festival","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Bernard Tschumi","work":"Parc de la Villette exploded axonometric","year":"1983","why":"points, lines and surfaces pulled apart into layers: the exploded axon as the clearest possible statement of a layered concept","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parc_de_la_Villette","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Rem Koolhaas","work":"Delirious New York axonometrics","year":"1978","why":"the skyscraper anatomised floor by floor in parallel projection, where perspective would have hidden the stacking","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delirious_New_York","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Constantine Anderson","work":"Axonometric map of Midtown Manhattan","year":"1985","why":"an entire city district drawn building by building at one consistent parallel angle: patience as projection","tier":"internal"}],"ai":{"helps":["Fast axon-flavoured illustrations for slides and early studies, where approximate parallelism is acceptable","Turning a rough massing or plan into an exploded-view aesthetic to storyboard a real drawing","Generating flat-colour isometric entourage (trees, furniture, vehicles) for collage into constructed axons"],"breaks":["Models re-insert vanishing points: trained overwhelmingly on photographs, they pull parallel edges toward convergence, and 'axonometric' quietly drifts into three-quarter perspective","The projection angle wanders across the image: the front of the drawing obeys one angle, the background another","Verticals tilt and true heights drift, so nothing can be measured along an axis, which was the entire point","Depth cues creep back in: distant objects shrink, blur and fade, betraying the mode's flatness"],"recipes":[{"label":"True plan axon","prompt":"Redraw this plan as a plan axonometric: rotate the plan 45 degrees keeping its geometry completely undistorted, then extrude all verticals straight up at consistent true heights. Strict parallel projection: every set of parallel edges remains exactly parallel across the entire image, including background objects; no vanishing points, no convergence anywhere. Uniform thin black linework, flat pale colour fills, white background. No shadows, no atmospheric depth, no blur, no size reduction with distance."},{"label":"Exploded layers","prompt":"Draw this design as an exploded axonometric separated into four layers: ground plane, structure, envelope, roof. Lift the layers vertically apart with thin dashed alignment lines connecting corresponding corners. Identical parallel projection angle for every layer, no perspective anywhere. Flat solid fills with one muted colour per layer, uniform thin black linework, white background, no shadows, no text."},{"label":"Scroll oblique","prompt":"Redraw this streetscape as a long horizontal oblique projection in the manner of a Chinese narrative handscroll: plan edges stay truly horizontal, depth recedes at a constant 45-degree angle, and there is no diminution: far objects are drawn the same size as near ones. Fine ink linework with muted mineral colour washes. No vanishing points, no photographic lighting, no depth blur."}]},"lensPack":[["Parallel edges","strict parallel projection: all parallel edges stay parallel, no vanishing points anywhere"],["45/45 plan axon","plan kept undistorted, rotated 45 degrees, verticals extruded at true height"],["Exploded axon","as an exploded axonometric, layers lifted apart, thin dashed alignment lines"],["No depth cues","no diminution with distance, no blur, no atmospheric fade; depth by geometry only"]],"examples":[{"id":"board-brooklyn-brownstone","title":"Text → A1 presentation board (Brooklyn brownstone renovation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-oma-community-centre","title":"Text → A1 presentation board (OMA-style community centre)","tier":"public"},{"id":"cad-to-exploded-axon","title":"Rhino screenshot → exploded isometric assembly diagram","tier":"public"},{"id":"massing-to-program-axon","title":"Massing render → colour-coded program axonometric","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-axon-illustration","title":"Building photo → clean axonometric illustration","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-analysis-diagrams","title":"Library render → circulation, exploded and analysis diagrams","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-axon-illustration","title":"Night render → clean axonometric illustration (glass-brick offices)","tier":"public"},{"id":"section-to-3d-isometric","title":"Landscape section → realistic 3D isometric","tier":"public"},{"id":"style-linework-to-illustration","title":"Linework isometric → flat colour illustration (palette input)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-sydney-opera-house","title":"Text → massing-evolution timeline (Sydney Opera House)","tier":"public"},{"id":"townhouse-plan-to-axon","title":"Townhouse landscape plan → 3D axonometric diagram","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"perspective-view","title":"Perspective View","tagline":"a view is an argument about where to stand","definition":"Perspective constructs how space appears from one chosen station point, with parallel lines converging to vanishing points as they do on the retina. That choice is never neutral: deciding where the viewer stands, at what height, looking at what, is deciding whose experience the drawing argues for. The single hero view is the weakest use of the mode; its real power is serial, in sequences that reconstruct movement through space the way Bacon storyboarded whole cities. Since the Renaissance, perspective has been both the most seductive and the most ideological drawing we make, and AI has inherited exactly that bias.","purpose":["Testing and communicating experience: what a person actually encounters at eye level","Arguing for a specific subject position: the child, the wheelchair user, the worker on the night shift","Narrating movement through sequences of views along an approach or path","Persuasion, used knowingly: the perspective is rhetoric, and should be authored as such"],"conventions":["A declared station point and eye height, normally about 1.6 metres for a standing adult","One horizon per image; all vanishing points sit on it","A stated lens: wide angles dramatise and distort, long lenses flatten and calm","Entourage at correct scale, doing plausible things; people are scale instruments before they are decoration","In series: fixed eye height and lens across frames, so only position changes"],"canon":[{"who":"Albrecht Durer","work":"Perspective machine woodcuts, Underweysung der Messung","year":"1525","why":"the apparatus laid bare: perspective shown as a mechanical procedure of threads and frames, not a mystery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Edmund Bacon","work":"Design of Cities movement sequences","year":"1967","why":"cities explained as chains of eye-level views: perspective made serial, and space made narrative","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Bacon_(architect)","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Superstudio","work":"The Continuous Monument","year":"1969","why":"deadpan one-point perspective as critique: the seductive view turned against the world it depicts","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superstudio","tier":"internal"},{"who":"NemeStudio","work":"Montage perspectives","year":"2010s","why":"contemporary perspectives built as flat, estranged montages: proof the mode need not mean photorealism","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Alvaro Siza","work":"Freehand design perspectives","year":"1970s-present","why":"quick eye-level perspectives, often with the designer's own hand or figures in frame: viewpoint as empathy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%81lvaro_Siza_Vieira","tier":"internal"}],"ai":{"helps":["This is AI's native mode: it produces convincing perspectival space, light and atmosphere on demand","Camera control by prompt: eye height, lens, position and framing can be specified and iterated cheaply","Serial studies: many viewpoints of one design quickly, to find which views carry the argument","Entourage, weather and season, which are tedious to build and cheap to generate"],"breaks":["Left alone it defaults to the drone shot and the golden-hour hero view: viewpoints nobody will ever occupy, chosen by the training data, not by you","Eye heights drift upward and lenses go wide, flattering the object and abandoning the pedestrian","Geometry wobbles: double horizons, inconsistent convergence, staircases and shadows that contradict the vanishing points","Style flattening: every perspective converges toward the same glossy render aesthetic, erasing the difference between your office and everyone else's"],"recipes":[{"label":"Argued eye-level view","prompt":"Render this design from a standing adult's eye level, exactly 1.6 metres above ground, positioned at the main entrance approach, with a 35mm lens equivalent and a single consistent horizon. Keep the layout, massing and materials exactly as in the source. Flat overcast light. A few people mid-stride at correct human scale, none looking at the camera. No drone views, no wide-angle distortion, no golden-hour drama, no lens flare."},{"label":"Sequence of approach","prompt":"Produce four frames of a walking approach to this building along its entry path: frame one from 80 metres away, frame two from 40, frame three from 15, frame four at the threshold. Identical eye height of 1.6 metres, identical lens and identical overcast light in all frames; the building geometry does not change between frames. Present the frames as one horizontal strip in walking order, separated by thin white gaps, no captions."},{"label":"Deadpan one-point","prompt":"Render a deadpan one-point perspective looking exactly down the central axis of this space: camera on axis at 1.6 metres, symmetrical framing, horizon at the vertical centre of the image. Flat even daylight, muted colour, empty of people. Keep all geometry from the source and add nothing outside the corridor of the view. No dramatic sky, no birds, no lens flare, no vignetting."}]},"lensPack":[["1.6 m eye","from a standing adult eye height of 1.6 metres, 35mm lens, single consistent horizon"],["Approach series","frames along the approach path, same eye height and lens, geometry unchanged"],["No hero shot","no drone view, no golden-hour drama, no lens flare; a view a person can actually occupy"],["On-axis deadpan","one-point perspective exactly on axis, symmetrical frame, flat even light"]],"examples":[{"id":"circulation-overlay","title":"Render → circulation overlay","tier":"public"},{"id":"cleanup-site-photo","title":"Clean up a site photo","tier":"public"},{"id":"context-cafe-human-activity","title":"Cafe render → populated with human activity","tier":"public"},{"id":"context-office-atmosphere","title":"Office render → winter / night / rain atmospheres","tier":"public"},{"id":"context-pavilion-trout-lake","title":"Pavilion render → placed at Trout Lake (real site)","tier":"public"},{"id":"context-villa-rainforest","title":"Villa render → tropical rainforest context (+ markup fig tree)","tier":"public"},{"id":"contours-to-terrain","title":"Contour map → terrain view","tier":"public"},{"id":"extend-canvas-outpaint","title":"Extend the canvas (outpainting)","tier":"public"},{"id":"material-kitchen-moodboard","title":"Kitchen render → material palette from a mood board","tier":"public"},{"id":"material-markup-mesh","title":"Markup circles → mesh facade on selected surfaces only","tier":"public"},{"id":"material-precedent-concrete","title":"Precedent reference → cast white concrete pavilion","tier":"public"},{"id":"material-washroom-swap","title":"Public washroom render → material swap by explicit prompt","tier":"public"},{"id":"perspective-to-cad-plan","title":"Perspective render → CAD-style plan drawing","tier":"public"},{"id":"perspective-to-rendered-plan","title":"Perspective render → photorealistic rendered plan","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-3d-model-museum","title":"Museum render → standalone 3D model (freeform museum)","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-3d-model-pavilion","title":"Pavilion photo → standalone 3D model (curved pavilion)","tier":"public"},{"id":"plan-to-interior-render","title":"Floor plan → interior render (view from the red arrow)","tier":"public"},{"id":"plan-to-townhouse-birdseye","title":"Landscape plan → bird's-eye render (townhouse complex)","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-axon-illustration","title":"Night render → clean axonometric illustration (glass-brick offices)","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-elevation-svg","title":"Bath house render → CAD-style elevation (+ SVG step)","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-figure-ground","title":"Render → figure-ground diagram","tier":"public"},{"id":"render-to-watercolour","title":"Render → watercolour","tier":"public"},{"id":"section-to-section-perspective","title":"Flat section → collage-style section perspective","tier":"public"},{"id":"streetview-redesign","title":"Street view → streetscape proposal","tier":"public"},{"id":"style-photoreal-to-collage","title":"Photorealistic render → Photoshop collage style","tier":"public"},{"id":"style-photoreal-to-red-linework","title":"Photorealistic render → red pen/linework illustration","tier":"public"},{"id":"view-exterior-to-aerial","title":"Bath house exterior → bird's-eye view","tier":"public"},{"id":"view-exterior-to-interior","title":"Bath house exterior → consistent interior view","tier":"public"},{"id":"view-markup-arrow","title":"Arrow markup → render from the marked viewpoint","tier":"public"},{"id":"villa-render-to-elevation-svg","title":"Villa render → CAD-style elevation (+ SVG step)","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"hybrid-drawing","title":"Hybrid Drawing","tagline":"one drawing, many mediums, many ideas","definition":"A hybrid drawing splices representational systems into a single image: measured linework against photography, flat colour against deep space, text against texture. The splice is the content; each medium keeps its own logic, and the visible seam between them is where the argument lives. James Corner called the strongest of these eidetic images: not pictures of a place but instruments that provoke ideas about it. The lineage is long, from Repton's Red Book overlay flaps, a two-hundred-year-old before/after edit, through Dada photomontage to the contemporary studio collage.","purpose":["Holding measurement and atmosphere in one image, when neither a plan nor a rendering alone can carry the idea","Speculation: montage lets the not-yet-possible sit convincingly inside the real","Layering knowledge systems, such as survey, photograph, diagram and text, over one another","Escaping the deadness of the seamless render by keeping the making visible"],"conventions":["Keep the seams: each medium retains its own logic, and the reader should see where systems meet","Contrast is structural: flat against deep, line against photo, exact against loose","Vector stays vector: measured linework remains crisp and unpainted","Sources are edited, not blended; hard silhouette cuts, not soft global filters","The image rewards two readings: an instant graphic impression and a slow decoding of layers"],"canon":[{"who":"Humphry Repton","work":"Red Books with before/after overlay flaps","year":"1790s","why":"lift the flap and the landscape transforms: the 200-year-old ancestor of generative fill, done with watercolour and hinged paper","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humphry_Repton","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Hannah Hoch","work":"Dada photomontages","year":"1919-1930s","why":"cutting photographs against each other as critique: the technical and political root of the collage lineage","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hannah_H%C3%B6ch","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Archigram","work":"Instant City","year":"1969","why":"airships, halftones, ads and drawing spliced into one propulsive image: montage as project, not illustration","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archigram","tier":"internal"},{"who":"David Hockney","work":"Joiners (photocollages)","year":"1980s","why":"dozens of photographs of one scene reassembled with visible seams: proof that fractured images can see more than seamless ones","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Hockney","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Lebbeus Woods","work":"War and Architecture (Sarajevo)","year":"1993","why":"cartography, hand drafting and photography spliced into one wounded image: the hybrid drawing pushed into ethical and political argument, where the seam between systems carries the violence of the site","tier":"internal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebbeus_Woods"},{"who":"James Corner","work":"Taking Measures Across the American Landscape","year":"1996","why":"the eidetic anchor: maps, aerial photographs and measures composited into images that think","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Corner","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Tatiana Bilbao Estudio","work":"Studio collages","year":"2010s-present","why":"collage used instead of renderings on principle, keeping projects open where the render would close them","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tatiana_Bilbao","tier":"internal"},{"who":"LCLA Office (Luis Callejas)","work":"Landscape montages","year":"2010s-present","why":"contemporary landscape montage: restrained splices of drawing and photograph that keep territory strange","tier":"internal"}],"ai":{"helps":["Generating individual ingredients (a sky, a texture field, a cut-out tree, a crowd) for you to collage by hand","Extending or in-filling photographic layers behind your linework, which is exactly the Repton flap operation","Producing before/after variants of a base photograph while your drawn layers stay untouched on top"],"breaks":["AI homogenises: diffusion wants one consistent image, so it melts the seams that make a hybrid a hybrid","It relights and restyles every layer to agree, destroying the structural contrast of flat against deep, line against photo","Measured linework does not survive: the vector layer gets painted, blurred and bent the moment it enters the model","Style transfer flattens a deliberate collision of mediums into a single fashionable 'collage look', which is the opposite of the mode"],"recipes":[{"label":"Ingredient, not the dish","prompt":"Generate a single ingredient for a collage, not a finished image: a flat, evenly lit photographic texture of dry meadow grass seen from directly above, filling the frame edge to edge. No objects, no horizon, no shadows of things outside the frame, no vignetting, no perspective. It will be cut and placed into a measured drawing by hand."},{"label":"Repton flap","prompt":"Using this photograph of the existing site, change only the paved forecourt into a wildflower meadow with a single mature oak; preserve everything else exactly: buildings, sky, light direction, camera position, and all surrounding context pixel-for-pixel where unchanged. The result should read as the 'after' leaf of a before-and-after pair, matched precisely to the 'before'."},{"label":"Keep the seams","prompt":"Compose an image that is deliberately three mediums at once: the ground plane as a measured black-and-white line drawing in parallel projection, the mid-ground trees and people as cut-out photographic collage elements with hard silhouette edges, and the sky as one flat unbroken colour field. Do not blend the mediums into one another; keep every seam sharp and visible; no unified lighting, no global shadows, no filter over the whole image."}]},"lensPack":[["Cut-out collage","as photographic cut-outs with hard silhouette edges placed over flat colour fields"],["Line plus photo","measured linework kept crisp and unpainted, photographic elements sitting on it as collage"],["Keep the seams","do not blend mediums; keep the splice visible, no unified lighting, no global filter"],["Texture ingredient","one flat texture field, edge to edge, no objects, made for later hand collage"]],"examples":[{"id":"section-to-section-perspective","title":"Flat section → collage-style section perspective","tier":"public"},{"id":"style-photoreal-to-collage","title":"Photorealistic render → Photoshop collage style","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"mapping","title":"Mapping","tagline":"maps do not describe territory, they make it","definition":"Mapping is not the neutral recording of ground; every map selects, schematises and projects, and those editorial acts shape what can be thought and built on the territory. James Corner's formulation is the mode's charter: the agency of mapping lies in what it uncovers and sets in motion, not in how faithfully it mirrors. A drainage map, a property map and a smell map of the same square kilometre are three different worlds. Designers map in order to find the project hiding in the territory, which makes the map a design act that precedes design.","purpose":["Finding the project in the territory: mapping to reveal forces, flows and claims that plans ignore","Making the invisible operative: floods, toxins, ownership, migration, memory","Arguing about power, since whoever draws the boundary defines the ground","Building a shared factual stage on which a proposal can be judged"],"conventions":["Declared projection, scale, orientation and source; a map that hides its construction hides its bias","A legend that closes: every mark on the map has a meaning, and every meaning appears in the key","Selection with a conscience: what is omitted must be a decision, not an accident","Data binds to location; symbol size, colour and position mean something checkable","Layering as argument: superimposed layers are a claim that these things belong together"],"canon":[{"who":"James Corner","work":"The Agency of Mapping","year":"1999","why":"the theoretical charter: mapping as a creative act that uncovers and constructs rather than copies","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Corner"},{"who":"Harold Fisk","work":"Geological maps of the Mississippi meander belt","year":"1944","why":"thousands of years of river movement compressed into ribboned colour: time made cartographic, and accidentally one of the most beautiful maps ever drawn","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Kate Orff (SCAPE)","work":"Petrochemical America (with Richard Misrach)","year":"2012","why":"maps and sections that indict: the throughline drawn from petrochemical infrastructure to bodies and landscapes","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kate_Orff","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Mark Lombardi","work":"Narrative structures (power network drawings)","year":"1990s","why":"finance and influence mapped as delicate pencil constellations: the map of relations with no geography at all","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Lombardi","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Giambattista Nolli","work":"Nuova Pianta di Roma","year":"1748","why":"the figure-ground pact: public interiors drawn as open space, private mass as poche; a map that redefined what counts as the city","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giambattista_Nolli"},{"who":"Desimini and Waldheim","work":"Cartographic Grounds","year":"2016","why":"the contemporary survey of the mode: projection, contour and hatch reclaimed as design instruments rather than GIS defaults","tier":"internal"}],"ai":{"helps":["Cartographic styling of a real base you supply: palettes, hatches, line vocabularies applied to true geometry","Terrain and texture aesthetics such as hillshade moods and material fields for backgrounds under accurate linework","Visual-language studies: testing how a mapping series could look before you build it with real data"],"breaks":["AI maps are pictures of maps: coastlines, streets and topography are hallucinated, and the result has the authority of cartography with none of its truth, which is the most dangerous failure in this atlas","Text garbles: place names, scales and legend entries come back as convincing alphabet soup","Data cannot bind to location: symbol sizes and colour ramps are decoration, not encoding","Projection and graticule are treated as ornament; the grid bends where the composition wants it to"],"recipes":[{"label":"Style a real base","prompt":"Restyle this GIS map export without altering its geometry in any way: rivers and water in a single flat blue, land as flat paper white, urban fabric as a fine grey hatch, all supplied linework kept in exact position. Remove nothing and add nothing: no invented roads, no new coastline detail, no relief shading, no labels of any kind. Flat printed-map aesthetic, no satellite realism, no 3D."},{"label":"Meander palette","prompt":"Redraw this river corridor drawing in the graphic language of the 1944 Fisk Mississippi meander-belt maps: successive historical channel positions as overlapping ribbon bands in distinct flat colours, the present channel strongest, older channels progressively paler. Keep the channel geometry exactly as supplied and invent no additional channels. White background, thin black border, no text, no relief, no satellite texture."},{"label":"Nolli ground","prompt":"Convert this city plan into a Nolli-style figure-ground map: private building mass as solid dark grey poche, all publicly accessible space left white, including streets, squares and the interiors of the public buildings I have marked. Keep every footprint in true position and proportion; do not merge, regularise or invent buildings. No labels, no trees, no shadows, no colour."}]},"lensPack":[["Flat map graphics","flat printed-map aesthetic, no satellite realism, no 3D relief, no labels"],["Nolli ground","private mass as solid poche, public space white, footprints in true position"],["Layered traces","historical states as overlapping flat colour ribbons, the newest strongest"],["No invention","keep the supplied geometry exactly; no invented roads, coastlines, names or symbols"]],"examples":[{"id":"board-waterfront-renewal","title":"Text → landscape board (waterfront renewal & erosion mitigation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"contours-to-terrain","title":"Contour map → terrain view","tier":"public"},{"id":"landscape-to-desk-model-topo","title":"Terrain model → layered topographic desk model","tier":"public"},{"id":"perspective-to-program-masterplan","title":"Perspective render → diagrammatic program masterplan","tier":"public"},{"id":"spreadsheet-to-tree-masterplan","title":"Species spreadsheet → tree-placement masterplan diagrams","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"section-perspective","title":"Section Perspective","tagline":"the cut and the life behind it, in one drawing","definition":"The section perspective welds two systems into one image: an orthographic cut, flat and measurable, with a perspective opening into the space beyond it. It shows what a building or landscape is made of and what it feels like at the same time, which no other single drawing can do. The discipline of the mode is holding the two systems apart: the cut plane stays true, black and dimensional while the world behind it recedes, glows and fills with people. For landscape and urban work it is indispensable, because it alone connects soil, structure, section and social life in one continuous argument.","purpose":["Linking construction to experience: the wall build-up and the room it makes, in one image","Explaining ground: soils, water tables, foundations and roots below the line people stand on","Selling a spatial idea honestly, with the technical cut keeping the atmosphere accountable","Studying interior climates and sections of terrain where plan and elevation both fail"],"conventions":["The cut plane is orthographic and stays orthographic: flat, true-scale, never bent into the perspective","Cut material reads as solid poche, conventionally black, and is never textured into realism","One perspective system beyond the cut, usually one-point, with a single horizon","The ground is cut too: strata, foundations and water table drawn with the same authority as walls","People and furniture at correct scale populate the cut spaces; the drawing is about occupation"],"canon":[{"who":"Paul Rudolph","work":"Section perspectives (Yale Art and Architecture Building)","year":"1958-1963","why":"the modern benchmark: ferocious ink sections whose cut discipline makes the spatial drama believable","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Rudolph_(architect)"},{"who":"ARO and dlandstudio","work":"A New Urban Ground (Rising Currents)","year":"2010","why":"the city section extended into the harbour: infrastructure, ecology and street life argued in one continuous cut","tier":"internal"},{"who":"SCAPE","work":"Oyster-tecture (Rising Currents)","year":"2010","why":"a landscape section perspective that made reef ecology legible and lovable enough to become policy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kate_Orff","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Design Earth","work":"Geostories","year":"2018","why":"the section perspective scaled to the planet: geological cuts as speculative storytelling","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Atelier Bow-Wow","work":"Graphic Anatomy section perspectives","year":"2007","why":"thin-line section perspectives dense with daily life: the mode's gentlest and most precise contemporary form","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atelier_Bow-Wow"}],"ai":{"helps":["Populating a correct section-perspective underlay with light, atmosphere, weather and people","Applying tone and material washes behind linework once you have fixed the geometry","Fast mood variants of one section, such as morning, night, winter and flood, over an unchanged cut"],"breaks":["This is the hardest mode for AI, because it must hold two projection systems in one image and it cannot: either the cut plane bends into the perspective or the depth flattens into elevation","The cut line wanders: elements are sliced in front of and behind the plane inconsistently across the sheet","Poche gets rendered: the flat black cut grows brick courses and concrete texture, losing its conventional meaning","Below-ground is invented freely, with strata, foundations and utilities placed as decoration, and scale figures disagree between the cut zone and the depth"],"recipes":[{"label":"Hold the cut","prompt":"Turn this line-drawing section perspective into an atmospheric drawing while preserving its geometry exactly. The cut plane stays a flat, true-vertical black poche band: do not bend it, thin it, or give it any texture. The space beyond the cut gains soft daylight, muted material colour and a few people at correct human scale. One-point perspective only in the space beyond; the cut edges remain strictly orthographic. Do not move, add or remove any structural line."},{"label":"Earth section","prompt":"Render the below-ground half of this section as layered earth: distinct horizontal strata in flat muted colours, root zones as fine linework, foundations kept as solid black poche exactly where drawn. The strata run flat and continuous to the drawing edges with no perspective. Above ground remains exactly as in the source. No photorealistic soil texture, no invented pipes or utilities, no text."},{"label":"Occupied section","prompt":"Populate this section perspective with life without touching its architecture: people sitting, walking and working at correct human scale inside the cut rooms, warm interior light in occupied spaces, cool daylight outside. Every drawn line is fixed: walls, floors, ground and the black section poche do not change. Add no furniture larger than a table, no vehicles, no text, no new openings."}]},"lensPack":[["Black cut band","the cut plane as flat solid black poche, never bent, never textured"],["Depth beyond","perspective only in the space beyond the cut; cut edges stay strictly orthographic"],["Strata below","below-ground as flat layered strata, foundations kept in solid poche"],["Add life only","people, light and atmosphere added; every drawn line preserved exactly"]],"examples":[{"id":"section-to-section-perspective","title":"Flat section → collage-style section perspective","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"model-photography","title":"Model & Model Photography","tagline":"the model is the argument; the photo is its portrait","definition":"The physical model is a representation in three dimensions, and photographing it is a second act of representation layered on the first. A chipboard massing model says something no render can: this is a proposition, tested by hand, still open to the knife. The photograph then chooses light, lens and distance to argue with the artifact, from Mies pasting model photos into real street photographs to Zumthor building at scales large enough that the camera can stand inside them. The genre's honesty is its rhetoric, which is exactly what makes its AI imitation philosophically slippery.","purpose":["Testing mass, proportion and light with the body and the hands, not the screen","Communicating stage-of-thinking: a rough foam model tells a client this is still open","Producing atmosphere without photorealism: model photos are evocative and honest at once","Composing the model into a real site photograph to test presence at urban scale"],"conventions":["Material declares intent: blue foam and chipboard mean study; basswood and museum board mean resolution","One consistent scale per model, with a scale figure in every photograph","Photograph at the model's implied eye level for spatial reading, or frankly from above for massing","Light is designed: a single hard source stands in for sun, and its angle is chosen, not accidental","Craft is legible: cut edges, joints and even repairs are evidence of testing, not flaws to hide"],"canon":[{"who":"Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","work":"Friedrichstrasse skyscraper photomontage and the collage-with-model tradition","year":"1921","why":"the model image spliced into the real city: the founding move of the genre and of architectural montage at large","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludwig_Mies_van_der_Rohe"},{"who":"OMA","work":"Blue-foam study models","year":"1990s-present","why":"cheap foam cut fast and photographed plainly: the model as a disposable instrument of thinking, not a precious artifact","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_for_Metropolitan_Architecture"},{"who":"Peter Zumthor","work":"Atmospheric large-scale material models","year":"1990s-present","why":"models built in the project's real materials and photographed like buildings: atmosphere tested physically before it exists","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Zumthor"},{"who":"Studio culture (anonymous)","work":"The chipboard model on the studio desk","year":"ongoing","why":"the vernacular genre itself: desk, daylight, glue and card; now so codified that AI can reproduce it convincingly, which is exactly the problem to teach"}],"ai":{"helps":["The 'photo of a chipboard model' is a well-represented genre AI reproduces convincingly, useful as an honest-looking register for early ideas","Pulling an overcooked render back into proposition-space by restyling it as a study model photograph","Cheap material dress rehearsals: the same massing as foam, card or basswood, before any blade is lifted"],"breaks":["Fake craft: glue seams, cut edges and fingerprints appear where no hand ever worked, an image wearing the costume of evidence","Scale cues contradict: wood grain too coarse for the implied scale, dust too fine, scale figures at the wrong size","Studio physics drift: depth of field, shadows and reflections that no desk lamp could produce","The ethical failure: a model photo asserts 'we built and tested this'; an AI model photo can quietly counterfeit that assertion, so generated model images must always be captioned as generated"],"recipes":[{"label":"Chipboard desk shot","prompt":"Re-render this design as a photograph of a physical study model: grey chipboard and basswood with visible cut edges and faint glue seams, sitting on a white studio desk beside a white card scale figure. Shot from desk level with a 50mm lens, shallow depth of field, soft window light from the left. Keep the design's massing and proportions exactly; simplify all detail the way a knife-cut model would: no material colours, no glazing reflections, no landscape realism, no miniature people."},{"label":"Foam massing series","prompt":"Show this massing as three blue-foam study models photographed side by side on kraft paper, identical camera and identical single hard light for all three. Each model varies only the roof form; footprint and height datum stay identical. Rough hot-wire-cut surfaces, no added detail, no scale figures, no text. The photograph is realistic but the models remain honestly foam: nothing photorealistic about the buildings themselves."},{"label":"Mies montage","prompt":"Composite this model photograph into the supplied site photograph in the manner of the 1921 Mies Friedrichstrasse photomontage: the model stays visibly a model, monochrome and simplified against the photographic grain of the street, while the site photo keeps its full reality. Match only the perspective, horizon and scale; do not blend materials, do not relight the model to match the scene, and keep the splice clearly legible."}]},"lensPack":[["Chipboard + basswood","as a photograph of a chipboard and basswood study model, visible cut edges, desk-level 50mm shot"],["Blue foam","as rough blue-foam massing models, hot-wire-cut surfaces, kraft paper base"],["White museum model","as a monochrome white presentation model, even soft light, seamless grey background"],["Honest artifact","the model must read as a model: card scale figure, glue seams, simplified detail"]],"examples":[{"id":"landscape-to-desk-model-topo","title":"Terrain model → layered topographic desk model","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-3d-model-brick-corner","title":"Street photo → standalone 3D model (brick corner building)","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-3d-model-museum","title":"Museum render → standalone 3D model (freeform museum)","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-3d-model-pavilion","title":"Pavilion photo → standalone 3D model (curved pavilion)","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-desk-model-millboard","title":"Building photo → millboard study model on a desk","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-desk-model-pvc","title":"Freeform building → white PVC study model on a desk","tier":"public"}]},{"id":"board-layout","title":"Board & Layout","tagline":"the sheet is the argument","definition":"Layout is the representation of representations: the art of organising drawings, images and text on a sheet so that the sequence of reading becomes the structure of the argument. A grid disciplines the page; hierarchy decides what is seen first, what supports, and what whispers in the captions. Muller-Brockmann treated the grid as an ethics of clarity, and the best boards still do: nothing placed by accident, nothing sized by whim. A weak project cannot be saved by layout, but a strong project is routinely lost by it.","purpose":["Turning a pile of drawings into one argument with a beginning, a middle and an end","Controlling first read: what a juror understands from three metres away in three seconds","Holding a multi-board or multi-page set consistent, so the system itself signals rigour","Pacing attention: sequence and scale changes as narrative devices, as Powers of Ten demonstrates"],"conventions":["A modular grid, declared and obeyed: columns, gutters and baselines govern every placement","Hierarchy in no more than three levels: hero, support, caption; if everything is loud, nothing is","Alignment is trust: edges that almost line up read as error from across the room","White space is structural, not leftover; emptiness directs the eye","Real type, set in layout software: typography is content, and it is never generated as pixels"],"canon":[{"who":"Josef Muller-Brockmann","work":"Grid Systems in Graphic Design","year":"1981","why":"the canonical manual: the grid as an instrument of order, economy and intellectual honesty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josef_M%C3%BCller-Brockmann","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Danne and Blackburn","work":"NASA Graphics Standards Manual","year":"1976","why":"system beats instance: a manual so disciplined it made an agency's paperwork feel like the space program","tier":"internal"},{"who":"James Corner and Alex MacLean","work":"Taking Measures Across the American Landscape (spreads)","year":"1996","why":"page architecture as argument: maps, aerial photographs and text choreographed across each spread","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Corner","tier":"internal"},{"who":"Chris Ware","work":"Building Stories; Jimmy Corrigan page structures","year":"2000s","why":"the most sophisticated reading hierarchies on paper: panel scale, position and rhythm doing the work of narration","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chris_Ware"},{"who":"Charles and Ray Eames","work":"Powers of Ten","year":"1977","why":"sequence as argument: one rule (a factor of ten) generating an irresistible visual narrative","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powers_of_Ten_(film)"}],"ai":{"helps":["Generating the images that go into the layout at a consistent style, so the board reads as one family","Grey-box mood studies of a board's visual tone and density before any real content exists","Testing hero-image crops and aspect ratios quickly before committing the grid"],"breaks":["Text garbling is fatal here: AI cannot set type, and titles, labels, scales and captions return as convincing alphabet soup on the one artifact where every word is read","It cannot hold a modular grid: gutters drift, columns wobble, alignments almost happen, and 'almost aligned' reads as error","It decorates instead of ranking: everything comes back at equal visual volume, and hierarchy, which is the entire content of layout, disappears","Consistency across a set fails: board two will not match board one in palette, weight or style. The rule: AI for imagery, InDesign for type and grid"],"recipes":[{"label":"Grey-box wireframe","prompt":"Produce a wireframe study of an A1 portrait presentation board using only grey rectangles on white: one dominant image block spanning two-thirds of the width at the top, a strip of four equal small frames below it, and a narrow text column on the right indicated as light-grey horizontal lines, not letters. Strict alignment to a visible six-column grid with even gutters. Absolutely no words, no letterforms, no pseudo-text glyphs anywhere."},{"label":"One graphic family","prompt":"Restyle these four drawings into one consistent graphic family for a single presentation board: identical white backgrounds, identical black line colour, one shared muted accent colour, the same lighting logic and the same level of abstraction across all four. Change styling only: keep each drawing's geometry, projection and content exactly as supplied. Output all four at identical aspect ratios, with no text on any image."},{"label":"Hero crop tests","prompt":"From this drawing generate three alternative crops for a board's hero image: one extreme horizontal panorama, one square detail crop, one full-bleed vertical. Crop only: do not redraw, restyle, extend or retouch anything; the geometry stays untouched. Present the three crops side by side on a white ground with thin black keylines and no captions."}]},"lensPack":[["Grey-box wireframe","layout study as grey rectangles on a visible column grid; no letterforms of any kind"],["One graphic family","identical background, line colour, accent and abstraction level across every image"],["No AI type","no words, labels, titles or pseudo-text anywhere; type is set in InDesign"],["Crop only","crop the image only; geometry untouched, thin black keyline, no captions"]],"examples":[{"id":"board-brooklyn-brownstone","title":"Text → A1 presentation board (Brooklyn brownstone renovation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-interior-renovation","title":"Interior photo → renovation presentation board","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-oma-community-centre","title":"Text → A1 presentation board (OMA-style community centre)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-street-renovation","title":"Text → landscape board (street renovation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"board-waterfront-renewal","title":"Text → landscape board (waterfront renewal & erosion mitigation)","tier":"public"},{"id":"photo-to-desk-model-millboard","title":"Building photo → millboard study model on a desk","tier":"public"},{"id":"renders-to-library-board","title":"Project renders → competition board (library)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-high-line","title":"Text → plan-view timeline (the High Line)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-oakridge-park","title":"Text → collage timeline (Oakridge Park redevelopment)","tier":"public"},{"id":"timeline-sydney-opera-house","title":"Text → massing-evolution timeline (Sydney Opera House)","tier":"public"}]}]};
